WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.939
A public archive adds three application workers
behind one service address.

00:00:04.939 --> 00:00:09.813
With round robin, six incoming requests can be
distributed two per worker.

00:00:09.813 --> 00:00:15.938
That sounds balanced until one request generates a
large export while the others read a small

00:00:15.938 --> 00:00:16.399
record.

00:00:16.399 --> 00:00:21.075
Equal request counts do not imply equal work or
equal completion times.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:27.544
A routing policy uses observable signals, and those
signals are only approximations of the capacity

00:00:27.544 --> 00:00:29.236
each worker has available.

00:00:29.236 --> 00:00:32.165
Next ask whether workers are interchangeable.

00:00:32.165 --> 00:00:38.608
If an export’s progress exists only in worker A’s
memory, sending a follow-up request to B may fail

00:00:38.608 --> 00:00:40.235
even though B is healthy.

00:00:40.235 --> 00:00:45.993
Durable progress in shared storage can allow
replacement, but that storage now needs its own

00:00:45.993 --> 00:00:47.746
capacity and failure design.

00:00:47.746 --> 00:00:53.191
The balancer gives clients a stable entry point and
can hide worker membership changes.

00:00:53.191 --> 00:00:56.946
It does not remove every dependency behind that
entry point.

00:00:56.946 --> 00:01:03.205
Follow one request to storage and back before
claiming that adding workers has multiplied end-to-
end

00:01:03.205 --> 00:01:03.768
capacity.

00:01:03.768 --> 00:01:09.139
The archive serves metadata and large scans through
the same HTTPS hostname.

00:01:09.139 --> 00:01:15.853
If routing must distinguish /records from /scans,
some component must inspect HTTP information.

00:01:15.853 --> 00:01:22.778
An ordinary IP-and-port decision cannot see that
distinction inside encrypted application traffic.

00:01:22.778 --> 00:01:29.563
An HTTP-aware reverse proxy can terminate TLS,
inspect the path and select an appropriate worker

00:01:29.563 --> 00:01:29.916
pool.

00:01:29.916 --> 00:01:36.252
It may establish a second encrypted connection
upstream; termination does not require leaving the

00:01:36.252 --> 00:01:37.885
internal hop unprotected.

00:01:37.885 --> 00:01:44.287
For a generic transport service, a layer-four
balancer may be a better fit because it routes using

00:01:44.287 --> 00:01:48.141
connection information without interpreting HTTP
resources.

00:01:48.141 --> 00:01:52.713
NAT is one implementation, not the definition of
layer-four balancing.

00:01:52.716 --> 00:01:59.023
Likewise, a reverse proxy need not wait for an
entire document before forwarding it: streaming and

00:01:59.023 --> 00:02:02.434
buffering depend on implementation and
configuration.

00:02:02.434 --> 00:02:08.612
Compare designs using the features required by this
workload, then benchmark realistic payloads,

00:02:08.612 --> 00:02:10.800
connections and security settings.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:16.141
Historical throughput examples should not become
universal limits for either layer.
